{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Our newest AI Quest: Mapping the human brain\nSource: GOOGLE AI BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nPolar Peaks: A journey to map the human brain\nTraining modules: Tools for teaching AI literacy\nGoogle AI Blog\nOur newest AI Quest: Mapping the human brain\nOctober 26, 2026\n2 min read\n\nToday, we’re launching “Polar Peaks”, our third quest in the AI Quests series, created by Google Research and the Stanford Accelerator for Learning to teach students how AI works and how to use it responsibly. AI Quests invites students ages 11 to 14 to step into the shoes of Google researchers to tackle some of humanity’s biggest challenges.\n\nMore than 400,000 quests have been completed to date, localized to 10 languages. Alongside the new quest, we’re also releasing training modules for teachers.\n\nPolar Peaks: A journey to map the human brain\n\nIn the first two quests, students tackle flood forecasting and eye disease detection, inspired by Google Research’s Flood Forecasting initiative and AI model for diabetic retinopathy screening. Our newest quest, “Polar Peaks,” has students travel to the frontiers of neuroscience and see how AI can speed up scientific research.\n\nWith the guidance of in-game mentors, students scan and map the neurons in the brain to better understand the game character’s memory loss. They complete tasks like data cleaning and model testing, learning firsthand that AI requires careful human oversight and critical thinking.\n\nThe quest is inspired by over a decade of our research in connectomics: the science of mapping neural networks in the brain. Upon completion of the quest, students hear from two researchers behind our real-world breakthroughs, including the recent mapping of the complete fruit fly brain, the largest brain map by number of neurons to date.\n\nTraining modules: Tools for teaching AI literacy\n\nTeachers play a critical role in bringing the quests to life and fitting them into their existing classroom curriculum. We’re rolling out new dedicated teacher training modules to help educators reinforce key AI literacy concepts and use AI Quests for learning and discussion. These are launching within the Google AI Educator Series. The modules for Quest #1 and #2 are accessible here. The training module for Quest #3 will follow next month.\n\nEarlier this year, AI Quests won a 2026 Webby Award for AI Education, Learning & Training, and Best Global K–12 Learning Solution at the ETIH Innovation Awards. We’ll continue working with educators and partners to bring more quests to more classrooms this school year.\n\nPosted in:\n\nPublished by Google AI Blog\nView original article ","response":"In this article\n\nPolar Peaks: A journey to map the human brain\nTraining modules: Tools for teaching AI literacy\nGoogle AI Blog\nOur newest AI Quest: Mapping the human brain\nOctober 26, 2026\n2 min read\n\nToday, we’re launching “Polar Peaks”, our third quest in the AI Quests series, created by Google Research and the Stanford Accelerator for Learning to teach students how AI works and how to use it responsibly. AI Quests invites students ages 11 to 14 to step into the shoes of Google researchers to tackle some of humanity’s biggest challenges.\n\nMore than 400,000 quests have been completed to date, localized to 10 languages. Alongside the new quest, we’re also releasing training modules for teachers.\n\nPolar Peaks: A journey to map the human brain\n\nIn the first two quests, students tackle flood forecasting and eye disease detection, inspired by Google Research’s Flood Forecasting initiative and AI model for diabetic retinopathy screening. Our newest quest, “Polar Peaks,” has students travel to the frontiers of neuroscience and see how AI can speed up scientific research.\n\nWith the guidance of in-game mentors, students scan and map the neurons in the brain to better understand the game character’s memory loss. They complete tasks like data cleaning and model testing, learning firsthand that AI requires careful human oversight and critical thinking.\n\nThe quest is inspired by over a decade of our research in connectomics: the science of mapping neural networks in the brain. Upon completion of the quest, students hear from two researchers behind our real-world breakthroughs, including the recent mapping of the complete fruit fly brain, the largest brain map by number of neurons to date.\n\nTraining modules: Tools for teaching AI literacy\n\nTeachers play a critical role in bringing the quests to life and fitting them into their existing classroom curriculum. We’re rolling out new dedicated teacher training modules to help educators reinforce key AI literacy concepts and use AI Quests for learning and discussion. These are launching within the Google AI Educator Series. The modules for Quest #1 and #2 are accessible here. The training module for Quest #3 will follow next month.\n\nEarlier this year, AI Quests won a 2026 Webby Award for AI Education, Learning & Training, and Best Global K–12 Learning Solution at the ETIH Innovation Awards. We’ll continue working with educators and partners to bring more quests to more classrooms this school year.\n\nPosted in:\n\nPublished by Google AI Blog\nView original article ","reasoning":"The text contains a complete article with multiple coherent paragraphs covering the launch of Polar Peaks, details about previous quests, neuroscience context, and teacher training modules. It is substantive and informative.","titlePath":["Content quality @upstream","Live Feed: every probed card renders a real article in the reader"],"testFile":"tests/contentQuality.spec.ts","retry":0,"timestamp":"2026-10-28T14:31:21.343Z"}
{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing: Key Differences and Use Cases\nSource: TESTOMAT BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\nWhat Is Integration Testing in Software Testing?\nContract Test vs Integration Test: Which Failures Do They Catch?\nWhen to Use Contract Testing and Integration Testing in the Test Pyramid\nAPI Testing Tools for Contract Testing and Integration Testing\nHow Testomat.io Fits Contract Testing and Integration Testing for QA Teams\nCommon Mistakes When You Implement Contract Testing\nBottom Line\nTestomat Blog\nContract Testing vs Integration Testing: Key Differences and Use Cases\nTetiana Khomenko\nOctober 21, 2026\n12 min read\n\nYour orders service calls the payment service. The payment team renames a response field and merges. Every unit test stays green and the integration suite reports the failure later. A study in the Journal of Systems and Software names poor communication of API changes as a core challenge of microservice teams. This contract testing vs integration testing guide compares both testing approaches for API and microservices teams and shows where both results belong. Keep on reading to discover key differences between contract testing and integration testing.\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\n\nContract testing checks that a consumer and a provider agree on the request and response format, with each side tested alone. Integration testing runs multiple components together with real dependencies. The two test types answer different questions:\n\nA contract test asks whether the payment service sends the fields that the orders service reads.\nAn integration test asks whether the orders service and the payment service work together with the database in a real flow.\n\nYou need both answers before a release, and the table shows how contract testing compares to integration testing.\n\nAspect\tContract testing\tIntegration testing\nScope\tThe agreement of a consumer and a provider\tSeveral components in a real flow\nWhat runs\tEach side alone, with a mock of the other side\tAll components together\nDependencies\tA contract file replaces the other service\tReal database and real services\nSpeed\tSeconds, like a unit suite\tMinutes, with a running environment\nWho writes it\tThe consumer team and the provider team\tDevelopers and QA engineers\nA failure shows\tA changed field or a changed status code\tWrong data or a timeout\nEnvironment\tA single service and a mock\tA full test environment\nCI stage\tEvery pull request of both services\tAfter the merge and on a schedule\n\nThe contract test vs integration test question has a short answer: a contract test protects the API agreement, and an integration test protects the real behavior in a test environment that mirrors production. The next sections explain each way of testing and show which failures each type catches.\n\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\n\nContract testing is a testing approach that checks that a service consumer and a service provider agree on their API. The contract is a predefined file with the expected request and response pairs. The consumer is the API client that sends requests, for example the orders service. The provider is the service that answers, for example the payment service. The Pact documentation describes contract testing as “testing an integration point by checking each application in isolation”.\n\nEach side works in isolation and reads the same contract file, so independent services can deploy at different times and development teams avoid compatibility issues at release time. Automated contract testing works well for REST APIs, and contract testing can also cover message queues. The contract covers only the fields the consumer reads. The provider can add new fields, and the contract test stays green. The provider renames or removes a field the consumer reads, and the contract test fails in the pull request of the provider. This early detection prevents integration failures that an end-to-end test would report days later.\n\nConsumer-Driven Contract Testing Wi …[truncated]","response":"In this article\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\nWhat Is Integration Testing in Software Testing?\nContract Test vs Integration Test: Which Failures Do They Catch?\nWhen to Use Contract Testing and Integration Testing in the Test Pyramid\nAPI Testing Tools for Contract Testing and Integration Testing\nHow Testomat.io Fits Contract Testing and Integration Testing for QA Teams\nCommon Mistakes When You Implement Contract Testing\nBottom Line\nTestomat Blog\nContract Testing vs Integration Testing: Key Differences and Use Cases\nTetiana Khomenko\nOctober 21, 2026\n12 min read\n\nYour orders service calls the payment service. The payment team renames a response field and merges. Every unit test stays green and the integration suite reports the failure later. A study in the Journal of Systems and Software names poor communication of API changes as a core challenge of microservice teams. This contract testing vs integration testing guide compares both testing approaches for API and microservices teams and shows where both results belong. Keep on reading to discover key differences between contract testing and integration testing.\n\nWhat Is the Difference: Contract Testing vs Integration Testing?\n\nContract testing checks that a consumer and a provider agree on the request and response format, with each side tested alone. Integration testing runs multiple components together with real dependencies. The two test types answer different questions:\n\nA contract test asks whether the payment service sends the fields that the orders service reads.\nAn integration test asks whether the orders service and the payment service work together with the database in a real flow.\n\nYou need both answers before a release, and the table shows how contract testing compares to integration testing.\n\nAspect\tContract testing\tIntegration testing\nScope\tThe agreement of a consumer and a provider\tSeveral components in a real flow\nWhat runs\tEach side alone, with a mock of the other side\tAll components together\nDependencies\tA contract file replaces the other service\tReal database and real services\nSpeed\tSeconds, like a unit suite\tMinutes, with a running environment\nWho writes it\tThe consumer team and the provider team\tDevelopers and QA engineers\nA failure shows\tA changed field or a changed status code\tWrong data or a timeout\nEnvironment\tA single service and a mock\tA full test environment\nCI stage\tEvery pull request of both services\tAfter the merge and on a schedule\n\nThe contract test vs integration test question has a short answer: a contract test protects the API agreement, and an integration test protects the real behavior in a test environment that mirrors production. The next sections explain each way of testing and show which failures each type catches.\n\nWhat Is Contract Testing?\n\nContract testing is a testing approach that checks that a service consumer and a service provider agree on their API. The contract is a predefined file with the expected request and response pairs. The consumer is the API client that sends requests, for example the orders service. The provider is the service that answers, for example the payment service. The Pact documentation describes contract testing as “testing an integration point by checking each application in isolation”.\n\nEach side works in isolation and reads the same contract file, so independent services can deploy at different times and development teams avoid compatibility issues at release time. Automated contract testing works well for REST APIs, and contract testing can also cover message queues. The contract covers only the fields the consumer reads. The provider can add new fields, and the contract test stays green. The provider renames or removes a field the consumer reads, and the contract test fails in the pull request of the provider. This early detection prevents integration failures that an end-to-end test would report days later.\n\nConsumer-Driven Contract Testing Wi …[truncated]","reasoning":"The text contains a substantive article body with multiple coherent paragraphs on a clear topic (contract testing vs integration testing), including sections with explanations, comparisons, and examples. Although truncated at the end, the content is informative and not a failure state.","titlePath":["Content quality @upstream","Live Feed: every probed card renders a real article in the reader"],"testFile":"tests/contentQuality.spec.ts","retry":0,"timestamp":"2026-10-28T14:31:31.852Z"}
{"judgeName":"articleQuality:body","judge":"Article Body Quality","judgeModel":"deepseek:deepseek-v4-pro","criteria":["Contains a real, meaningful article body (not an error, fallback notice or junk).","Scores 4-5 pass; 1-3 fail."],"score":5,"maxScore":5,"passingScore":4,"passed":true,"prompt":"Title: How to Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack | mabl\nSource: MABL BLOG\n\nIn this article\n\nKey Takeaways\nWhy Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack?\nAgentic Development Tech Stack Layers at a Glance\nLayer 2: Orchestration, Planning, and Agent Workflows\nLayer 3: Tools, MCP, and Action Interfaces\nLayer 4: Memory, Context, and Retrieval\nLayer 5: Guardrails, Permissions, and Governance\nLayer 6: Observability and AI Agent Evaluation\nLayer 7: Testing and Independent Verification\nAgentic Development Tech Stack Checklist Before Production\nHow mabl Fits Into the Agentic Development Tech Stack\nMabl Blog\nHow to Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack\nAbbey Charles\nOctober 20, 2026\n8 min read\nKey Takeaways\nAn agentic development tech stack combines models, orchestration, tools, memory, guardrails, observability, evaluations, and testing for production use.\nAI and code models power agent reasoning and code generation. The surrounding stack provides context, tools, permissions, workflow controls, and independent verification.\nTesting should sit beside evaluations and observability to verify generated code across real product, user, and system workflows.\nStrong stacks give agents clear tasks and controlled access while people retain authority over risk, governance, and release decisions.\n\n\nWhat is an agentic development tech stack? It's the set of systems that help AI agents complete software work safely and reliably.\n\nThe stack can include AI models, orchestration, tools, memory, guardrails, observability, evaluations, and testing.\n\nTogether, these layers help AI agents plan work, modify code, use development tools, and respond to feedback.\n\nThey also give your team control over access, risk, approvals, verification, and release decisions. Those controls become more important as agentic development changes how code is created, reviewed, tested, and released.\n\nModels and prompts may start the process. Production use depends on the systems surrounding them.\n\nAn agentic testing platform brings independent verification into the stack from the beginning.\n\nWhy Build an Agentic Development Tech Stack?\n\nCoding assistants can suggest functions, explain errors, or generate small changes.\n\nAgentic workflows handle longer sequences of work throughout the delivery process.\n\nAn agent may read a ticket, inspect several files, call tools, run checks, and prepare a pull request. Each step depends on reliable context, controlled access, and clear feedback.\n\nWithout a defined stack, those responsibilities often become scattered across separate tools and team-owned integrations:\n\nPermissions may vary\nContext can get lost\nActions become harder to trace\nTesting may arrive too late\n\nAn agentic development tech stack brings those systems into one operating model. It connects the tools agents use with the controls, evidence, and quality checks your team needs.\n\nOnce agents can modify code or trigger delivery systems, teams need clear approval paths and reliable verification. They also need a record of what changed, what the agent did, and how the outcome was validated.\n\nA well-designed stack gives you consistent control across the workflow. You can limit higher-risk actions, route decisions for approval, and verify outcomes before release.\n\nAgentic Development Tech Stack Layers at a Glance\n\nThe layers below show the systems needed to support agentic development from initial reasoning through final verification.\n\nStack Layer\n\n\t\n\nWhat It Does\n\n\t\n\nCommon Examples\n\n\n\n\nModels\n\n\t\n\nProvide reasoning, generation, and code assistance.\n\n\t\n\nLLMs, code models, multimodal models\n\n\n\n\nOrch